WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:06.060
As history often teaches us, sometimes human
beings are the biggest monsters of all.

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:11.930
Of course, when you join the ranks of the
SCP Foundation, that starts to seem even less

00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:12.930
likely.

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:14.040
And why wouldn’t it?

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:17.640
After all, just look at some of the creatures
they encounter and keep in containment.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:23.689
A giant unkillable lizard, a being with toothy
protrusions growing out of its body, a whole

00:00:23.689 --> 00:00:28.949
host of immortal eldritch beings that could
destroy reality, and one sadistic old man

00:00:28.949 --> 00:00:33.809
who drags unsuspecting victims into his own
personal hellscape.

00:00:33.809 --> 00:00:39.290
Monsters are a dime a dozen to the Foundation,
humanity could never be anywhere near as evil

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:43.640
or as cruel as the real monsters, even on
their worst day.

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:46.289
Or at least, you’d think.

00:00:46.289 --> 00:00:50.760
Researcher Keltzen had held much of the same
mentality, that the SCP Foundation had the

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:54.510
job of defending humanity from the real monsters
of the world.

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:59.230
And it was because of that belief that he
had never previously questioned the Containment

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:02.710
Procedures in place for SCP-1000.

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:08.000
These beings were a species of nocturnal and
omnivorous apes, usually with bodies covered

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.299
in either grey, brown, black, red or white
fur.

00:01:12.299 --> 00:01:17.310
Possessing the same level of intelligence
as the common chimp, the SCP-1000s had been

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:22.280
colloquially described as a species known
as ‘Bigfoot’, linking them to the urban

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:25.850
legend about an ape-like being of dubious
existence.

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:30.400
Keltzen had hardly been surprised that Bigfoot
itself was an anomalous SCP.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:34.909
There were already a number of others that
the Foundation had encountered that seemed

00:01:34.909 --> 00:01:37.960
to have influenced popular folklore from different
parts of the world.

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:43.229
In fact, it was that unique sub-category of
anomaly that Keltzen was conducting research

00:01:43.229 --> 00:01:46.649
into when he came across the SCP-1000 files.

00:01:46.649 --> 00:01:53.770
He was trying to determine, in various cases,
which came first: the anomaly, or the legend?

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:59.340
Did the existence of SCP-1000s inspire earlier
human civilisations to pass on stories of

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:00.549
the Sasquatch?

00:02:00.549 --> 00:02:07.990
Or had those word of mouth tales been somehow
to the very origins of SCP-1000 themselves?

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:13.569
Studying the files, Keltzen knew there was
a memetic disease infecting hominids like

00:02:13.569 --> 00:02:14.569
SCP-1000.

00:02:14.569 --> 00:02:18.480
The infection meant that anyone who observed
one of the Bigfoot creatures had at minimum

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:21.989
a 2% chance of suffering instantaneous brain
death.

00:02:21.989 --> 00:02:27.650
It would make discovering the link between
the Sasquatch legends and SCP-1000 difficult,

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:29.820
not being able to directly look at the apes.

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:35.340
But Keltzen hoped he wouldn’t need to actually
observe the creatures, only find evidence

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:41.599
that proved which predated which: SCP-1000
or the legend of Bigfoot.

00:02:41.599 --> 00:02:46.360
The population of SCP-1000 was limited, although
their highest concentrations were known to

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:50.840
be found in the Pacific Northwest region of
North America, as well as in the Himalayan

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:52.909
Mountain range in Asia.

00:02:52.909 --> 00:02:57.600
Being stationed at a Foundation facility in
the US, Researcher Keltzen opted to mount

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.849
an expedition to the former location.

00:02:59.849 --> 00:03:05.310
A few odd occurrences had taken place leading
up to his departure: his car tires had been

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:07.490
slashed, and now needed repairing.

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:11.959
Newspaper clippings were also left on Keltzen’s
desk that told of various hikers’ deaths

00:03:11.959 --> 00:03:14.190
taking place in the Pacific Northwest.

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:19.190
A more paranoid person might interpret these
as subtle, indirect threats; it certainly

00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:23.989
seemed as if someone in the SCP Foundation
wasn’t keen on Keltzen departing for his

00:03:23.989 --> 00:03:25.459
research trip.

00:03:25.459 --> 00:03:30.890
Nevertheless, he changed tact and called in
sick to work, sneaking away to a small airstrip

00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:36.230
and boarding a light cargo plane that smuggled
him towards the forests of North America.

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:38.920
Once he had arrived, night was already falling.

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:44.150
Keltzen was on his own, traversing the woods
as the sun rapidly receded out of view behind

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:49.400
the treeline, leaving the forest in darkness,
save for the beam from his flashlight.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:54.790
The researcher was hardly an outdoorsy type
and far from an expert at exploring the wilderness,

00:03:54.790 --> 00:04:00.569
but he pushed on into the heart of the woods,
to the areas where the most sightings of SCP-1000

00:04:00.569 --> 00:04:01.900
had been reported.

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:06.280
It wasn’t long before the torchlight fell
over something imprinted in the woodland floor:

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:11.439
a large footprint, left by something bipedal
and rather similar to an ape.

00:04:11.439 --> 00:04:16.139
Venturing further, the sound of twigs breaking
caused Keltzen to whirl around.

00:04:16.139 --> 00:04:21.000
The noise had come from nearby, making it
louder than the low, background ambiance of

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:22.290
the forest at night.

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:26.310
He swung his flashlight in the direction he
thought it had come from, only seeing the

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:29.270
shadows of branches cast against the tree
trunks.

00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:35.490
Then, another noise, leaves rustling as something
passed by, followed by more snapping of twigs,

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:37.949
then the shaking of the leaves again.

00:04:37.949 --> 00:04:42.020
This thing, whatever it might’ve been, was
encircling him.

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:44.710
Frantically, Keltzen tried to spot it with
his light.

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:50.139
When, suddenly, the beam fell over something
tall, a hulking figure moving between the

00:04:50.139 --> 00:04:51.160
trees.

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:56.110
Even from that split second sighting, it was
clearly an instance of SCP-1000.

00:04:56.110 --> 00:05:01.310
The momentary glimpse had reminded the intrepid
researcher of an important detail: he couldn’t

00:05:01.310 --> 00:05:02.310
look at them.

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:07.181
A 2% chance of instant brain death seemed
too low to worry about, but Keltzen’s research

00:05:07.181 --> 00:05:13.110
had described the effects of the memetic disease
to be cumulative: the longer an SCP-1000 ape

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:18.370
was observed, the higher the chance of being
killed on the spot by the infection.

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:23.210
Snapping his eyes shut as tight as he could,
Keltzen was frozen to the spot, at the mercy

00:05:23.210 --> 00:05:25.360
of the sounds surrounding him.

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:31.180
The SCP-1000 was drawing closer, enough for
the researcher to detect its footsteps.

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:34.590
His pulse quickened, along with his breathing
to match.

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:38.630
Although he was already aware that the apes
weren’t known for being aggressive, but

00:05:38.630 --> 00:05:42.120
hearing its heavy breathing as it drew nearer
was enough to make him uncertain.

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:47.940
Keltzen wasn’t sure if the Bigfoot would
attack him, or pass him by, but by far the

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:50.060
last thing he had expected…

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:52.780
was to hear it start speaking.

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:56.139
“Hu… man,” came a deep, gravely voice.

00:05:56.139 --> 00:06:00.810
It was the kind of sound an animal makes that
can almost be mistaken for speech.

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:05.570
Keltzen wasn’t fully convinced it had been
a word, rather just his ears playing tricks

00:06:05.570 --> 00:06:08.000
on him in a moment of heightened fear.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:10.400
“Human,” it repeated, much clearer.

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:17.250
There was no mistaking it a second time: the
SCP-1000 had spoken.

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:18.250
“Y-you can talk?”

00:06:18.250 --> 00:06:20.340
Keltzen exclaimed in disbelief.

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:25.400
The Sasquatch didn’t answer directly, instead
the researcher detected the sound of receding

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:26.400
footsteps.

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:30.500
There was a pause, as the SCP-1000 ape turned
back to Keltzen.

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:32.970
“Follow, human,” it said.

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:35.669
“Open your eyes and follow me.”

00:06:35.669 --> 00:06:37.720
“What about the disease?”

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:40.710
Keltzen shouted, keeping his eyelids still
screwed shut.

00:06:40.710 --> 00:06:45.400
“You’ll be safe,” the ape replied, before
it resumed walking away.

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:49.620
Cautiously opening his eyes and lifting his
flashlight, Keltzen could see the hairy figure

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:53.300
making its way through the forest, its back
to him.

00:06:53.300 --> 00:06:58.690
He could see it; the researcher could look
directly at it and yet nothing seemed to be

00:06:58.690 --> 00:06:59.690
happening.

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:02.830
No adverse side effects, no instant brain
death.

00:07:02.830 --> 00:07:07.351
The odds were low, Keltzen reminded himself,
as he began to warily follow the path of the

00:07:07.351 --> 00:07:08.351
ape.

00:07:08.351 --> 00:07:13.690
Following at a distance, he made sure to momentarily
glance away, trying not to stare for too long

00:07:13.690 --> 00:07:20.270
at the SCP-1000 creature, in the hope that
might disrupt the memetic effects of the infection.

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:24.060
Before long they had arrived at a cave, deep
in the heart of the forest.

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:28.009
The embers of a small fire were still aglow
near the rocky entrance.

00:07:28.009 --> 00:07:34.051
As they lumbered inside, the SCP-1000 threw
extra wood on the fire, poking it with a long

00:07:34.051 --> 00:07:37.199
stick until the flames began to reach higher.

00:07:37.199 --> 00:07:41.129
The flickering orange gave Keltzen a better
look at the ape, its large frame, covered

00:07:41.129 --> 00:07:43.350
almost head to toe in brown fur.

00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:49.590
There was something about the creature that
seemed off, not unsettling but almost sad,

00:07:49.590 --> 00:07:50.810
melancholic…

00:07:50.810 --> 00:07:52.199
like it was lonely.

00:07:52.199 --> 00:07:56.110
The researcher had been so amazed that he
could observe the creature for so long without

00:07:56.110 --> 00:08:02.540
dying that he’d barely focused in on the
more important discovery: SCP-1000s were far

00:08:02.540 --> 00:08:08.300
more intelligent than the Foundation files
had indicated, capable of not only speech,

00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:10.070
but emotion too.

00:08:10.070 --> 00:08:11.810
“D-do you have a name?”

00:08:11.810 --> 00:08:14.139
Keltzen stammered, unsure where to start.

00:08:14.139 --> 00:08:15.780
“Grykor,” it replied.

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:17.440
“That is my name.”

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:22.340
“You’ll have to forgive me, I’m a little
taken aback by all this,” the researcher

00:08:22.340 --> 00:08:23.460
hastily explained.

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:27.389
“You’re able to articulate, your species
is advanced enough to have names.”

00:08:27.389 --> 00:08:29.699
“Why should that be surprising?”

00:08:29.699 --> 00:08:30.900
Grykor asked.

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:34.980
“Well, it’s just, the Foundation seems
to think you and your kind are…

00:08:34.980 --> 00:08:38.890
I don’t mean any offense but, mindless beasts.”

00:08:38.890 --> 00:08:44.560
The ape made a huffing, exhaling sound; Keltzen
noticed after a moment that they were chuckling,

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:47.260
a dry, almost sarcastic chuckle.

00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:51.560
It was unlike anything he had expected to
learn about SCP-1000.

00:08:51.560 --> 00:08:55.610
That reminded him of why he’d come here.

00:08:55.610 --> 00:08:59.620
“Grykor, may I ask something?” he cleared
his throat.

00:08:59.620 --> 00:09:04.470
Nodding, the SCP-1000 instance poked at the
fire again, paying little mind to the human

00:09:04.470 --> 00:09:05.540
in its company.

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:08.030
“Where did you and your people come from?”

00:09:08.030 --> 00:09:09.210
Keltzen asked.

00:09:09.210 --> 00:09:10.690
“What’s your history?”

00:09:10.690 --> 00:09:11.690
“Why?”

00:09:11.690 --> 00:09:13.080
Grykor replied abruptly.

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:20.570
“You see, humans have these legends… stories
about something called Bigfoot, or a Sasquatch.

00:09:20.570 --> 00:09:24.050
The Foundation, the humans I work with, they
call you one and the same.

00:09:24.050 --> 00:09:26.570
I wanted to know if that was true.”

00:09:26.570 --> 00:09:28.670
For a short moment, the creature paused.

00:09:28.670 --> 00:09:33.290
Then, Grykor began to give Keltsen the truth;
the real truth.

00:09:33.290 --> 00:09:38.260
And as they spoke, it was as if the researcher
could see the story unfolding, emphasized

00:09:38.260 --> 00:09:40.940
by the pain in the ape’s teary eyes.

00:09:40.940 --> 00:09:45.700
“We were like you, long, long ago,” it
began explaining.

00:09:45.700 --> 00:09:50.250
“We and humans, we evolved together, side
by side.

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:53.130
For a time, there was peace between us.

00:09:53.130 --> 00:09:58.760
But we began to grow faster, stronger, became
smarter while you were left behind.”

00:09:58.760 --> 00:10:01.740
“You evolved before early humans did?”

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:03.140
Keltzen probed.

00:10:03.140 --> 00:10:05.600
“Indeed,” Grykor answered with a gentle
nod.

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:08.290
“We began to surpass the humans.

00:10:08.290 --> 00:10:14.959
You were still young, still hunting and foraging
for food while we were moving far beyond.

00:10:14.959 --> 00:10:19.690
Thousands and thousands of years, we were
able to develop our culture, our technology,

00:10:19.690 --> 00:10:24.310
all before the humans had even come close
to fire and the wheel.”

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:26.310
“But that doesn’t make any sense!”

00:10:26.310 --> 00:10:28.200
the researcher interjected yet again.

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:32.121
“If that was the case, mankind would never
have had its chance to reach the top of the

00:10:32.121 --> 00:10:33.121
food chain.

00:10:33.121 --> 00:10:36.600
We would have died out, and that would have
made you the dominant species on the planet.”

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:38.490
“We were,” the SCP-1000 ape replied.

00:10:38.490 --> 00:10:40.180
“For a time.”

00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:42.570
“What happened?”

00:10:42.570 --> 00:10:45.620
Grykor turned with a solemn look on their
face.

00:10:45.620 --> 00:10:48.079
“You,” it replied plainly.

00:10:48.079 --> 00:10:49.680
“Humans happened.

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:53.290
We covered the world, billions and billions
of us.

00:10:53.290 --> 00:10:55.929
You were rare, as few as we are now.

00:10:55.929 --> 00:11:02.209
My people made technology far beyond anything
humans have dreamt up; either before or since.

00:11:02.209 --> 00:11:06.740
Trains, ships, vehicles created from the trees
and the animals.

00:11:06.740 --> 00:11:10.880
Communication using parts of insects and smaller
creatures.

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:16.070
Cities filled with us, where we all lived
in harmony, stretched out across parts of

00:11:16.070 --> 00:11:18.600
the world humans dared not to tread.”

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:21.290
“Organic technology,” Keltzen gasped.

00:11:21.290 --> 00:11:22.290
“Fascinating!”

00:11:22.290 --> 00:11:23.889
“We had weapons too.

00:11:23.889 --> 00:11:26.230
And that was our biggest mistake.”

00:11:26.230 --> 00:11:28.380
“What kind of weapons?”

00:11:28.380 --> 00:11:30.000
the researcher quizzed the ape.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:33.750
“Given how advanced you were compared to
humanity at that time, I’m assuming your

00:11:33.750 --> 00:11:36.480
weaponry was far beyond our early spears and
clubs.”

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:42.660
“The devastating kind,” Grykor said, staring
ahead into the fire, almost as if the dancing

00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:47.960
flames conjured up images of the kind of destruction
his ancestor’s weapons could cause.

00:11:47.960 --> 00:11:51.030
“I think I understand,” Keltzen said.

00:11:51.030 --> 00:11:53.839
“There was some kind of conflict, yes?

00:11:53.839 --> 00:11:58.200
A majority of your people perished… oh,
my condolences.

00:11:58.200 --> 00:12:02.320
But that would have removed you from the top
of the evolutionary chain, and allowed the

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:06.120
earliest ancestors of humanity to usurp your
place there?

00:12:06.120 --> 00:12:11.230
Was the disease you and your species carry
a result of that war, a sort of lingering

00:12:11.230 --> 00:12:13.670
radioactive fallout but on a genetic level?”

00:12:13.670 --> 00:12:17.170
“We are not sick,” Grykor growled.

00:12:17.170 --> 00:12:19.360
“Your people lied.

00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:21.940
They made that up.

00:12:21.940 --> 00:12:23.650
And there was no war…

00:12:23.650 --> 00:12:25.400
it was a slaughter.”

00:12:25.400 --> 00:12:29.180
Keltzen fell quiet, unsure of what to say.

00:12:29.180 --> 00:12:31.690
“Humans would stay away from us back then.

00:12:31.690 --> 00:12:33.270
We avoided them too.

00:12:33.270 --> 00:12:35.760
You slept during the night when we were awake.

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:37.300
But you made a deal.

00:12:37.300 --> 00:12:41.269
A deal with something ancient and dangerous
in the forests.

00:12:41.269 --> 00:12:43.570
A deal to even the score.

00:12:43.570 --> 00:12:47.190
You found our weapons… and didn’t hesitate.”

00:12:47.190 --> 00:12:53.040
“No, that’s…” the Foundation researcher
tried to protest, but the look of sadness

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:56.230
on the ape’s face stopped his sentence before
he could finish.

00:12:56.230 --> 00:13:01.150
“We were almost entirely wiped out, in a
single day.

00:13:01.150 --> 00:13:06.320
Humans switched places with us, we became
less and they became more.

00:13:06.320 --> 00:13:11.710
They used our technology to forget about us,
wiped their memories so they wouldn’t have

00:13:11.710 --> 00:13:13.750
to face up to what they had done.

00:13:13.750 --> 00:13:15.149
But we never forgot.”

00:13:15.149 --> 00:13:21.329
“And the Foundation, they maintain that
façade,” Keltzen realised.

00:13:21.329 --> 00:13:26.170
“That’s why they tell us you’re infected,
that if we look at you’ll we’ll die.

00:13:26.170 --> 00:13:27.690
They’re ashamed, we…

00:13:27.690 --> 00:13:28.740
we all should be.”

00:13:28.740 --> 00:13:32.390
“Not just ashamed,” Grykor replied.

00:13:32.390 --> 00:13:33.390
“Afraid.

00:13:33.390 --> 00:13:39.920
They fear we want revenge, retribution for
what was done to our people.”

00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:41.389
“And do you?”

00:13:41.389 --> 00:13:46.740
The ape took another pause, as if it was pondering…
or just allowing itself a moment to feel sad.

00:13:46.740 --> 00:13:50.459
“We have forgiven you,” it answered.

00:13:50.459 --> 00:13:55.930
“I’m not sure we deserve that,” Kletzen
said back, as the pair sat by the fire until

00:13:55.930 --> 00:13:57.750
the sun came up.

00:13:57.750 --> 00:14:04.459
Now go and check out “SCP-323 WENDIGO SKULL”
and “SCP-023 BLACK SHUCK” for more anomalous

00:14:04.459 --> 00:14:07.730
entities that are pulled straight out of folklore,
myths and ancient legends!

